.Dd November 3, 2026
.Dt MIXER 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mixer
.Nd read or set the volume, the input levels and the record source
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Nm
.Ar name
.Nm
.Ar name
.Ar N | L : Ns Ar R | Cm + Ns Ar N | Cm - Ns Ar N
.Nm
.Cm =rec
.Ar source
.Nm
.Cm mute | unmute
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads and sets the levels of the audio driver in use, Intel HDA or the
Sound Blaster 16, and the source it records, through
.Pa /dev/mixer ,
OSS's mixer device; see
.Xr mixer 4 .
A level is a percentage for each of the left and the right channel; the
device keeps one level for both, the louder of the two.
.Pp
A channel is named as OSS names it:
.Cm vol ,
the volume;
.Cm pcm ,
the level of the calling program's own playback, which for
.Nm
itself, playing nothing, reads 100 and does not change;
.Cm speaker ,
the level of the console's bell on a codec with a beep generator, such as
the Lenovo G50-70's, where 0 silences it;
.Cm mic ,
.Cm monitor
and
.Cm line ,
the microphone jack, the internal microphone and the line in;
.Cm rec ,
the record level, which scales each of the three; and
.Cm igain ,
the boost of the microphones' pins.
The device has only the channels its hardware has; the Sound Blaster 16
has
.Cm vol
and
.Cm pcm
alone.
.Pp
With no argument,
.Nm
prints each channel as
.Ql Ar name Ar L : Ns Ar R ,
followed by
.Ql \ muted
after the volume while it is muted and
.Ql \ rec
after the source recorded.
With a
.Ar name
alone it prints that channel.
.Bl -tag -width "=rec source"
.It Ar name Ar N
Set both channels to
.Ar N ,
0 through 100.
.It Ar name Ar L : Ns Ar R
Set the left channel to
.Ar L
and the right to
.Ar R .
.It Ar name Cm + Ns Ar N , Ar name Cm - Ns Ar N
Move both channels up or down by
.Ar N ,
keeping each between 0 and 100.
.It Cm =rec Ar source
Record from
.Ar source ,
one of
.Cm mic ,
.Cm monitor
and
.Cm line
that the device has, as FreeBSD's
.Xr mixer 8
spells it.
.It Cm mute
Record the volume's level in
.Pa /var/run/mixer.mute
and set it to 0.
Muting a muted volume changes nothing.
.It Cm unmute
Set the recorded level back and remove the record.
Unmuting a volume that is not muted changes nothing.
.El
.Pp
OSS has no mute of its own, so the record is the mute: the volume is
muted while the record is there and the level is 0.
A level set with
.Cm vol
removes the record, so that a step up from a mute starts from 0, and a
later
.Cm mute
records the new level.
.Pp
The levels set are kept across a halt, a power-off or a reboot, which
.Xr init 8
saves them at, and lost to a power cut; the record source is not kept.
.Pp
acpid's volume actions run
.Nm
for the volume keys and the mute key; see
.Xr init 8 .
The device is open to every account, as a desktop's access list opens it
on Linux, but only root can write the record, so another account can set
the level and cannot mute.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /var/run/mixer.mute
.It Pa /dev/mixer
The mixer device, present while an audio driver with a volume is in use.
.It Pa /var/run/mixer.mute
The level before a mute, as
.Ar L : Ns Ar R .
.Xr init 8
keeps it across a reboot with the level, so that a volume muted at the
halt is still muted after it.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 on success, 1 when the device or the record cannot be used, and
2 for a wrong usage, a channel the device lacks, a level that is not one
or a source that is not one.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mixer vol 50
mixer vol +5
mixer mute
mixer =rec mic
mixer mic 80
mixer
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ioctl 2 ,
.Xr mixer 4 ,
.Xr init 8
